🟢 DAY 20 — 
   HAVING
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

HAVING is used to filter groups created by GROUP BY.


1. WHERE VS HAVING

WHERE → Filters individual rows
HAVING → Filters groups


2. GROUP BY + HAVING

Find departments whose average salary is greater than 3000:

SELECT DEPTNO, AVG(SAL)
FROM EMPLOYEE
GROUP BY DEPTNO
HAVING AVG(SAL) > 3000;


3. HAVING + COUNT()

Find departments having more than 3 employees:

SELECT DEPTNO, COUNT(*)
FROM EMPLOYEE
GROUP BY DEPTNO
HAVING COUNT(*) > 3;


4. WHERE + GROUP BY + HAVING

Find departments having more than 2 employees whose salary is greater than 3000:

SELECT DEPTNO, COUNT(*)
FROM EMPLOYEE
WHERE SAL > 3000
GROUP BY DEPTNO
HAVING COUNT(*) > 2;

Process:

WHERE
↓
Filters rows

GROUP BY
↓
Creates groups

COUNT()
↓
Calculates

HAVING
↓
Filters groups


5. HAVING + SUM()

Find departments whose total salary is greater than 10,000:

SELECT DEPTNO, SUM(SAL)
FROM EMPLOYEE
GROUP BY DEPTNO
HAVING SUM(SAL) > 10000;


6. HAVING + MAX()

Find departments whose maximum salary is greater than 5000:

SELECT DEPTNO, MAX(SAL)
FROM EMPLOYEE
GROUP BY DEPTNO
HAVING MAX(SAL) > 5000;


